me{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a754ea4-a620-52e3-9e45-eb1a439094b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598857} :answer-field/id #uuid "a85173f5-dac2-5444-8947-ecd521c4115b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05eca5d1-264d-534b-bb43-d9e82d5ae8f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-341345" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x+1$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{4}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-2x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72da2a03-f669-5d0d-8417-d067a28f77c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{4}(y-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598896} {:answer/id #uuid "90eeb241-69f8-5085-bf61-209abd269c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}(y-7)^{2}(y+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598935} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b045a5-a943-5fbb-a8c6-1ccff1a2f0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}(y+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598973} {:answer/id #uuid "a8201c94-3e5c-5595-a87e-38f2ad75af91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}(y-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598975} {:answer/id #uuid "af7e2f0c-45de-5133-9574-1ee548fe61cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}(y+7)(y-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8201c94-3e5c-5595-a87e-38f2ad75af91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}(y-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598975} :answer-field/id #uuid "c569f020-fdae-502b-85c5-ec15eb12a098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fc37691-67df-54c1-b6bc-537caf407b7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-69843" :question/problem "Factor the expression ${y}^{5}-14{y}^{4}+49{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${y}^{3}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${y}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5}-14{y}^{4}+49{y}^{3} & ={y}^{3}({y}^{2}-14y+49)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression ${y}^{2}-14y+49$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}({y}^{2}-14y+49) & = \\\\ {y}^{3}({y}^{2}-14y+{7}^{2}) & = \\\\ {y}^{3}({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 7\\cdot y+{7}^{2}) & = \\\\ {y}^{3}(y-7)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "714cfbe9-2d13-5b8b-98df-5ab8d5c7be56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "714cfbe9-2d13-5b8b-98df-5ab8d5c7be56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603505} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ccbeb76-7843-553c-92cd-be2ac1c20cf7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c24a9d93-b4c5-53ca-904d-e2e2a5296272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c24a9d93-b4c5-53ca-904d-e2e2a5296272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603689} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2ce972e-fcf7-5a97-b735-84b9e7ba8a63" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4313f64-2573-50c2-904b-02ea0e396b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4313f64-2573-50c2-904b-02ea0e396b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603790} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e26bc2-b2d4-58d1-82c9-4f916287e7a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186603961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cdcb1d6-2659-5f3a-a18c-0574a457dc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-249962" :question/problem "The equation of the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+6x-8$ in vertex form is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$(x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}+$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square. \n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+6x-8=-({x}^{2}-6x+8)\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+8 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+8 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+8 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+8 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+8 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+8 & = \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-6x+8) & = \\\\ -((x-3)^{2}-1) & = \\\\ -(x-3)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is $y=-1(x-3)^{2}+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "296289b8-7562-55de-8fd9-7d7ec187e9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-\\sqrt{4w+9}}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604252} {:answer/id #uuid "5108f549-60a9-5264-8a24-77b7f78fd363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+2\\sqrt{4w+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604303} {:answer/id #uuid "8f82002a-66bc-574a-bf9d-97fd1f556847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+2\\sqrt{4w+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604366} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcb8f05-08ae-5810-9cb8-86a8a98974ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+\\sqrt{4w+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604386} {:answer/id #uuid "f3534e4d-c3ed-5ede-bbda-d39f6d680d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-3+\\sqrt{4w+9}}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fcb8f05-08ae-5810-9cb8-86a8a98974ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+\\sqrt{4w+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604386} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a73afe-29cc-5585-b271-aa7d2c9cf5a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ba5b2a7-d8c0-5071-b040-a175d1fe9291" :question/math-academy-id "q-85377" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{4w}{3+\\sqrt{4w+9}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $3-\\sqrt{4w+9}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $3-\\sqrt{4w+9},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4w}{3+\\sqrt{4w+9}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4w}{3+\\sqrt{4w+9}}\\cdot \\frac{3-\\sqrt{4w+9}}{3-\\sqrt{4w+9}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4w(3-\\sqrt{4w+9})}{(3+\\sqrt{4w+9})(3-\\sqrt{4w+9})} & = \\\\ \\frac{4w(3-\\sqrt{4w+9})}{{3}^{2}-(\\sqrt{4w+9})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4w(3-\\sqrt{4w+9})}{9-(4w+9)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4w(3-\\sqrt{4w+9})}{-4w} & = \\\\ -(3-\\sqrt{4w+9}) & = \\\\ -3+\\sqrt{4w+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325104e1-7434-5610-9b5f-7322edfa6ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186605345} {:answer/id #uuid "67cbcb9f-e0d7-52b8-a0cf-a54745470d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186605439} {:answer/id #uuid "a36f54f3-77ce-56ea-86ec-27df4bb7aca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186605550} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e5e441-b6c9-5c3a-8754-bc5251114629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186605589} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcff5bc-b10e-5bfa-939f-bdbb1dc328af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186605711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a36f54f3-77ce-56ea-86ec-27df4bb7aca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186605550} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aad0ff1-cef8-5eba-bfc4-134e470f6627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186605736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bb8ea4f-1260-58c0-ad1f-fdecef385850" :question/math-academy-id "q-43477" :question/problem "$\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{7})}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division.  So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{7})} &=\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $\\frac{2}{7}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\frac{7}{2}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{2}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{7}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 7}{3\\times 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{7})}=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf29cf1f-105d-59ed-acab-1d2b32137429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf29cf1f-105d-59ed-acab-1d2b32137429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f20c1dc-5375-587a-9f53-f4a8a233ae48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b36ac8b-e1cf-5d44-8149-0fdd05b34471" :question/math-academy-id "q-186280" :question/problem "$-6-(-8)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-8$ from $-6,$ we combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6-(-8) & = \\\\ -6+8 & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/0a/0a5ae43f1803cd1a29b48d94e3a9fcd2a0828501acf8686f5e261c9b977eedca.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bb8909e-03d7-55bc-851b-11c80faac82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607109} {:answer/id #uuid "3c281a0e-f3d0-518e-b0be-73991c3fe4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607126} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0ca4d0-4cce-5d5b-b7a1-b74fc851e23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-410" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607360} {:answer/id #uuid "ddddb72c-89da-5a5a-bea7-53ca3b60751